WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:06.020
If the CW's "Crisis on Infinite Earths" crossover
left you a little confused, don't worry.

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:07.240
You're not alone.

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:11.940
The good news is that we're here to sort out
a few of its more convoluted aspects and explain

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:13.880
just what was going on, and why.

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:19.840
Who exactly is the guy who shows up in Purgatory
and bestows seemingly unfathomable cosmic

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:21.240
powers on Oliver Queen?

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:26.240
He identifies himself as Jim Corrigan, the
Spectre, but if you haven't had your nose

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:30.720
buried in DC Comics for the better part of
your life, that probably didn't answer any

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:31.220
questions.

00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:36.730
Despite being a relatively obscure character,
the Spectre is a favorite for this kind of

00:00:36.730 --> 00:00:41.460
world-shattering event, because he's the literal
embodiment of the Wrath of God.

00:00:41.989 --> 00:00:47.580
He's less of a person and more of a force
of nature, a vessel for divine fury with cosmic

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:49.620
power even Superman could only imagine.

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:54.240
Unfortunately, it turned out that turning
God's vengeance into a person without any

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:57.300
mitigating force could lead to some abuses
of power.

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:03.780
To temper his vengeance and create a new superhero
in 1940 the Presence bound the Spectre to

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:09.540
Jim Corrigan, a cop who had been killed by
criminals, returning him to life as a ghostly

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:10.040
superhero.

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:14.940
Eventually, Corrigan worked off enough of
his sins that he was allowed to move on to

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:19.440
his final reward, and the "Spectre Force"
was passed down to others.

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:25.080
After his death, Green Lantern Hal Jordan
was the Spectre for a while, and then Gotham

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:28.080
City detective Cris Allen was drafted into
the job.

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:32.700
It's never been handed off to Oliver Queen
in the comics, but the fact that both characters

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:37.080
wear costumes built around green hoods makes
the shift in wardrobe an easy one.

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:41.520
Most heroes only get one chance to sacrifice
their lives to save the world.

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:46.860
Oliver Queen got two: once as Green Arrow,
fighting to the death against the Anti-Monitor's

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:52.340
army of shadows, and then again as the Spectre,
seemingly sacrificing his life to end the

00:01:52.340 --> 00:01:53.620
threat to the multiverse.

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:58.720
The Anti-Monitor's death didn't really stick,
though, so is Oliver really dead?

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:00.580
"You remember what I told you?"

00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:04.980
"Dying is the easy part."

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:09.140
The answer, most likely, is a frustrating
one: yes and no.

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:14.580
Oliver's definitely dead he's the Spectre,
and as the name implies, the Spectre is quite

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:15.880
literally a ghost.

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:21.200
That said, as far as superheroes are concerned,
death is not a permanent state.

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:26.210
The real question is whether Oliver Queen
will stick around in his new phantasmal form,

00:02:26.210 --> 00:02:29.220
and the answer to that is "probably."

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:34.390
If he's the Spectre, that means he's beyond
the traditional boundaries of life and death,

00:02:34.390 --> 00:02:37.920
meaning that he has a pretty good chance of
coming back as the driving force of a future

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:42.690
CW crossover, if Stephen Amell ever gets bored
with whatever he's going to do after eight

00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:46.560
seasons of Arrow, with a distinct possibility
he'll jump to professional wrestling.

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:52.700
One of the major selling points for the CW
"Crisis" was that it included nods to the

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:55.600
entire history of DC Comics films and TV shows.

00:02:55.820 --> 00:03:00.980
Some, like the Smallville scene or the visit
to Earth-99, were pretty relevant to the plot,

00:03:00.990 --> 00:03:05.490
while others were basically just cameos, and
they might've gone by too fast for viewers

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:09.980
who don't have vast portions of Who's Who
in the DC Universe memorized to keep up with.

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:14.880
This is another reference to the original
Crisis on Infinite Earths comic, which also

00:03:14.890 --> 00:03:19.911
opens with the destruction of a handful of
DC's long-standing parallel worlds, complete

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:21.900
with the red skies and fade to white.

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:27.480
While the comic starts off by obliterating
Earth-3, the show opens up with two incarnations

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:33.480
of the Dark Knight being wiped out of existence:
Earth-89 and Earth-66, setting the trend of

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:36.860
Earths mostly being numbered the years that
they debuted.

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:41.840
Neither of the Earths the event opens with
actually show that world's version of its

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:43.300
main character, Batman.

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:49.340
On Earth-89, we catch up with intrepid reporter
Alex Knox and Danny Elfman's classic score,

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:53.020
letting us know we're in the world of the
1989 Batman film.

00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:59.200
After that, we skip over to the decidedly
retro Earth-66, where Burt Ward appears with

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:04.000
a color scheme that lets us know we're seeing
the Robin of the classic Batman TV series.

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:06.500
"Holy crimson skies of death!"

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:12.700
We also see the Huntress running around on
rooftops on Earth-203, a reference to the

00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:16.760
Birds of Prey series which aired its single
season back in 2003.

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:19.680
There are a few notable exceptions to the
year rule, though.

00:04:19.960 --> 00:04:24.260
For instance, we see a Superman killed by
Lex Luthor on Earth-75.

00:04:24.270 --> 00:04:26.620
It's actually a reference to a single comic.

00:04:26.620 --> 00:04:31.920
The shot of Superman and Lois that we see
on the news is lifted from 1994's Superman

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:37.700
#75, the climax of the famous "Death of Superman"
story in which the Man of Steel was killed

00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:38.700
by Doomsday.

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:43.720
When the Superman of Earth-96 joins up with
the crew, there are a few jokes made about

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:48.410
how weird it is that he looks exactly like
Ray Palmer, although no one ever bothers to

00:04:48.410 --> 00:04:49.460
give a reason why.

00:04:50.070 --> 00:04:54.920
The obvious real-world reason is that both
characters are played by Brandon Routh, who

00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:58.140
starred as the Man of Steel in 2006's Superman
Returns.

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:04.220
Superman-96 is pretty clearly meant to be
the same Superman played by Christopher Reeve

00:05:04.220 --> 00:05:07.360
in the 1978 Superman film and its three sequels.

00:05:07.820 --> 00:05:12.040
And Routh has played that character before:
Superman Returns was meant to pick up after

00:05:12.040 --> 00:05:14.660
Superman II, ignoring the last two movies.

00:05:15.270 --> 00:05:20.400
When he sees Lois, we get a brief excerpt
of "Can You Read My Mind," the love theme

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:21.700
from Superman: The Movie.

00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:23.860
And then there's this line:

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:29.120
"Actually, this is the second time I've gone
nuts and fought myself."

00:05:30.220 --> 00:05:35.620
That's a callback to Superman III, where Superman
splits into Clark Kent and Superman to fight

00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:37.260
himself in a junkyard.

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:42.880
But why is he on Earth-96 and not Earth-206
or Earth-78?

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:46.060
Routh's Man of Steel isn't just from the movies.

00:05:46.580 --> 00:05:51.880
It's also a clear reference to Mark Waid and
Alex Ross's landmark 1996 comics miniseries

00:05:51.880 --> 00:05:53.140
Kingdom Come.

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:57.530
The deaths of Lois Lane and the rest of the
Daily Planet staff at the hands of the Joker

00:05:57.530 --> 00:06:02.920
are lifted from that story, as is the black-and-red
emblem that Superman wears in mourning.

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:07.990
One of the grimmest moments of "Crisis On
Infinite Earths" comes when Supergirl and

00:06:07.990 --> 00:06:10.300
Batwoman take a visit to Earth-99.

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:14.680
Kevin Conroy, best known for his starring
voice role in Batman: The Animated Series

00:06:14.680 --> 00:06:20.300
starting in 1992, plays a version of Batman
who has given into his darkest impulses.

00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:26.490
Bruce-99 appears to be in an incredible amount
of pain, both mental and physical, but we

00:06:26.490 --> 00:06:29.860
don't get a lot of details as to what happened
to make him this way.

00:06:29.860 --> 00:06:34.950
He does mention that his world's version of
Kate Kane had been killed in action as Batwoman,

00:06:34.950 --> 00:06:36.440
helping push him over the edge.

00:06:36.950 --> 00:06:41.440
Luke Fox tells Supergirl that many of his
injuries are the result of his fight with

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:44.620
Superman, though that doesn't seem to be the
entire truth of the matter.

00:06:45.020 --> 00:06:50.640
Like Superman-96, Bruce-99 takes his look
from Kingdom Come, where he's depicted wearing

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:52.220
a similar exoskeleton.

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:57.300
In their annotations to that story, Waid and
Ross told readers that there was no single

00:06:57.300 --> 00:07:02.160
event that caused this level of injury, and
that it was just the toll of years and years

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:05.450
of getting in multiple fistfights with Gotham's
weirdest villains.

00:07:05.450 --> 00:07:11.280
The 99, by the way, is probably a reference
to Batman Beyond, which premiered in 1999

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:15.920
and where Conroy voiced an older Bruce who
was a bit worn down, but not villainous.

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:21.860
In recent memory, Tom Welling has played Clark
Kent longer than anyone else, starring in

00:07:21.860 --> 00:07:23.750
ten seasons of Smallville.

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:27.700
But he only once donned Superman's red-and-blues
throughout the show's run.

00:07:28.060 --> 00:07:31.840
So it makes sense that when the heroes of
the Arrowverse catch up with Smallville's

00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:35.580
Clark on Earth-167, he still isn't Superman.

00:07:35.980 --> 00:07:40.760
Much to Lex Luthor's disgust, Clark actually
gave up his powers to focus on starting a

00:07:40.760 --> 00:07:41.920
family with Lois.

00:07:42.620 --> 00:07:45.200
"You gave up your powers?"

00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:46.380
"Can't say I miss these chats."

00:07:46.520 --> 00:07:48.040
"You were basically a god.

00:07:48.760 --> 00:07:49.820
You could fly!"

00:07:49.880 --> 00:07:55.660
This particular plot point seems to be loosely
inspired by Alan Moore and Curt Swan's "Whatever

00:07:55.660 --> 00:07:59.900
Happened to the Man of Tomorrow," the final
Superman story before the end of the original

00:07:59.900 --> 00:08:01.620
Crisis on Infinite Earths comics.

00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:06.320
In that story, it's revealed that Superman
gave up his powers by exposing himself to

00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:11.400
Gold Kryptonite, which permanently strips
Kryptonians of their metahuman abilities.

00:08:11.580 --> 00:08:15.820
It's pretty safe to assume that Smallville's
Clark did the same, especially since Gold

00:08:15.820 --> 00:08:19.000
Kryptonite was introduced in Smallville's
last season.

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:24.940
The comic book Crisis on Infinite Earths streamlined
DC's comic book multiverse which had spread

00:08:24.940 --> 00:08:31.650
out over worlds including Earth-2, Earth-3,
Earth-X, Earth-C, Earth-C-Minus, and so on

00:08:31.650 --> 00:08:33.420
into one single universe.

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:39.140
The TV version has done the same, putting
the main CW shows into one cohesive Earth-Prime.

00:08:39.660 --> 00:08:44.580
One of the reasons that DC had such a convoluted
multiverse is that unlike their competition

00:08:44.590 --> 00:08:48.930
at Marvel, where the universe and many of
its core characters had been created by a

00:08:48.930 --> 00:08:54.000
relatively small group of people and focused
mainly on New York, the DC universe was much

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:55.320
more of a patchwork.

00:08:55.720 --> 00:09:00.300
The characters had mostly been created in
isolation, sometimes by different publishers

00:09:00.300 --> 00:09:02.600
who were acquired by DC after the fact.

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:04.460
The same goes for the shows.

00:09:04.760 --> 00:09:10.480
Arrow came first and spun off directly into
Flash, Legends of Tomorrow, and Batwoman,

00:09:10.480 --> 00:09:15.271
but Supergirl originally aired on CBS, and
was completely separate from the CW shows

00:09:15.280 --> 00:09:18.260
until a crossover established it as a parallel
world.

00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:22.840
Black Lightning started off on the CW, but
it was set in a different universe in hopes

00:09:22.840 --> 00:09:27.340
of establishing its lesser-known hero outside
of the shadow of the other characters.

00:09:27.740 --> 00:09:32.540
With Supergirl, Flash, Batwoman, Legends of
Tomorrow, Black Lightning and Arrow now combined

00:09:32.550 --> 00:09:37.250
into the newly christened Earth-Prime, there's
a shared history, creating a world where all

00:09:37.250 --> 00:09:39.340
the characters exist alongside each other.

00:09:39.700 --> 00:09:45.060
So after eight years of Arrow, we finally
have a world where Superman and Batman coexist

00:09:45.060 --> 00:09:49.660
with the other heroes, finally making the
DC TV universe feel whole.

00:09:50.080 --> 00:09:54.080
Characters can interact without the need for
another world-shattering interdimensional

00:09:54.080 --> 00:09:54.580
crossover...

00:09:55.200 --> 00:09:58.760
"I mean, how often does the world almost come
to an end?"

00:10:02.180 --> 00:10:03.260
"Oh, it's like that."

00:10:03.940 --> 00:10:08.780
...so it's likely that we could see more instances
of the shows crossing over in the future.

00:10:09.080 --> 00:10:13.440
It's a lot easier to have the Flash hang out
with Supergirl when he doesn't have to vibrate

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:14.760
to a parallel world.

00:10:15.300 --> 00:10:20.260
The DC TV Multiverse has been at the center
of several crossovers that have become an

00:10:20.260 --> 00:10:20.820
annual tradition.

00:10:21.280 --> 00:10:26.220
A major character on The Flash, Harrison Wells,
is built entirely around swapping out with

00:10:26.230 --> 00:10:31.110
his own interdimensional counterparts every
now and then, forcing poor Tom Cavanagh to

00:10:31.110 --> 00:10:34.960
do a new accent every couple of months while
ostensibly playing the same guy.

00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:39.520
With that in mind, it seems like the shows
would be giving up a lot of potential stories

00:10:39.520 --> 00:10:42.500
with everything combined into the new Earth-Prime.

00:10:43.240 --> 00:10:47.460
But unlike the comic, the shows didn't actually
ditch the idea of the Multiverse.

00:10:47.960 --> 00:10:53.730
The main CW shows have all been added to a
single Earth, with the in-story reason being

00:10:53.730 --> 00:10:58.649
that they're the homeworlds of the seven "Paragon"
characters who saved the entirety of existence,

00:10:58.649 --> 00:11:00.720
but there are plenty of other worlds out there.

00:11:01.220 --> 00:11:05.830
In addition to confirming that the DC films
are a part of this multiverse through a surprise

00:11:05.830 --> 00:11:11.620
cameo from movie Flash Ezra Miller, it also
preserves some of the other shows.

00:11:11.620 --> 00:11:17.480
The shows streaming on DC Universe make it
through, with Earths 9, 19, and 21 being established

00:11:17.480 --> 00:11:21.620
as the settings for Titans, Swamp Thing, and
Doom Patrol, respectively.

00:11:22.240 --> 00:11:28.020
Earth-96 is shown to survive with Brandon
Routh's older Superman, and we also see Earth-12,

00:11:28.020 --> 00:11:31.980
hinted at being the setting for HBO Max's
upcoming Green Lantern show.

00:11:32.420 --> 00:11:37.500
For longtime comic book readers, the most
interesting reveal might be the all-new Earth-2,

00:11:37.500 --> 00:11:40.720
which will be the home of the upcoming Stargirl
show.

00:11:41.410 --> 00:11:45.720
In the comics, Earth-2 was the home of the
original Golden Age heroes of the Justice

00:11:45.720 --> 00:11:50.800
Society of America, who debuted in the '40s,
and, as time went on, their descendants.

00:11:51.320 --> 00:11:56.980
Stargirl herself was a reimagining of one
of those Earth-2 characters, the Star-Spangled

00:11:56.980 --> 00:12:02.110
Kid, and with the show drawing on the histories
of JSA characters such as Wildcat and Doctor

00:12:02.110 --> 00:12:04.920
Mid-Nite, setting it there makes a lot of
sense.

00:12:05.350 --> 00:12:09.880
It allows her world to have a different history,
playing into the idea of legacy that's so

00:12:09.900 --> 00:12:14.640
core to her character, without interfering
with the new "history" of Earth-Prime.

00:12:15.100 --> 00:12:20.260
While the original comics Crisis is largely
known as the crossover that got rid of Earth-2,

00:12:20.260 --> 00:12:24.500
the TV version is the one that has introduced
it to a whole new generation.

00:12:24.780 --> 00:12:26.740
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:12:26.920 --> 00:12:30.920
Plus, even more Looper videos about your favorite
superhero shows are coming soon.

